wlanpi-mcp (0.7.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * tls: the SSE transport is now served over HTTPS on port 8767. The package
    installs an nginx site (/etc/wlanpi-mcp/nginx/wlanpi_mcp_tls.conf) that
    terminates TLS with the self-signed device certificate wlanpi-core
    generates (/etc/nginx/ssl/self-signed-wlanpi.*) and proxies to the MCP
    daemon on loopback. postinst links it into /etc/nginx/sites-enabled via
    wlanpi-mcp-nginx-setup.sh, which validates the nginx config and unlinks
    again on failure so nginx (shared with wlanpi-core) is never left broken;
    postrm removes the link. Clients connect to https://<device>:8767/sse.
  * The daemon now binds to 127.0.0.1 by default (WLANPI_MCP_HOST); the
    cleartext port 8766 is no longer reachable from the LAN. On upgrade,
    postinst moves a still-default WLANPI_MCP_HOST=0.0.0.0 in
    /etc/wlanpi-mcp/config.env to 127.0.0.1 and leaves any other value alone.
  * ufw: rules version 2. The application profile now defines wlanpi-mcp-tls
    (8767/tcp), which is opened, and keeps wlanpi-mcp (8766/tcp) defined but
    closed; the setup script deletes the version-1 'allow wlanpi-mcp' rule on
    upgrade. Both setup scripts take environment overrides so they can be
    exercised in tests.
  * debian: depend on nginx and ufw explicitly; ship /var/log/wlanpi-mcp for
    the front-end's access/error logs. Actually install
    wlanpi-mcp-ufw-first-boot.service, which postinst enables inside a chroot
    (image build) but which was never listed in wlanpi-mcp.install, so image
    builds never applied the firewall rules on first boot.
  * tests: new tests/test_tls_packaging.py covers the nginx site, the UFW
    profile, both setup scripts (against temp dirs and stub ufw/ischroot),
    the maintainer scripts and the loopback defaults.

 -- Jake Snyder <jsnyder81@gmail.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

wlanpi-mcp (0.6.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * capture: capture_scan/capture_observe now also save the raw pcapng to the
    managed capture directory (Settings.PCAP_CAPTURE_DIR) while returning the
    dissected summary, and report it as 'pcap_path'/'pcap_bytes'. This lets the
    streaming summary be verified against the frames with fetch_pcap_file. The
    saved file uses the same naming as the file-capture tools, so it is also
    listed by list_pcap_files and fetchable by capture_id. Teeing is
    best-effort, so a storage problem never breaks a capture.
  * capture: factor the capture directory, filename scheme and path-confinement
    check into wlanpi_mcp/capture/storage.py, shared by both the streaming tee
    and the file-capture tools.

